<!-- Pose Studio IK handles (#530): the bone chains that get a draggable handle.
       joints   root -> end. Every joint before the last is solved, the last one keeps
                (or takes) the handle's orientation.
                The handle sits where the WORN RIGGED MESH ends the limb: of the joints at
                and below the end joint that a worn rigged mesh weights vertices to, the
                one whose vertex region reaches farthest from the chain's root, at that
                region's far end (so a hoof's handle is on the hoof even when the wrist or
                ankle is the "knee"). Joint-position overrides and scale are included. The
                choice is logged at INFO ("IK chain ...") when Pose Studio shows the handles
                and whenever the outfit's rigging changes.
       tip      fallback when nothing worn is rigged at / below the end joint (system
                avatar, mesh still loading): the first of these joints the avatar has.
       hand_branches, hand_depth   a hand: when at least hand_branches children of the end
                joint carry rigged fingers, the search stops hand_depth joints below it, so
                a human hand's handle sits at the knuckles instead of a fingertip. A hoof
                rigged on the hand bones uses fewer branches and is not capped.
       rigged   1: only shown when a worn rigged mesh binds the chain (at or below it)
       group    wings: shown by "IK wings" (SLPoseStudioIKWings, default on) instead of
                the Bento setting
       solver   twobone  a limb with an elbow / knee (pole) handle on its second joint:
                         analytic two-bone IK for 3 joints, FABRIK (every joint folds,
                         the pole turns the bend plane) when the chain is longer or has
                         grown to its hoof (extend). Only the root keeps its dial ranges
                         under "IK limits"; the hinges are capped by max_bend.
                ccd      tail, wings, ears: FABRIK (analytic for 3 joints) with every joint
                         held to its dial ranges, then damped CCD to close what the limits
                         allow
                lookat   aim the last joint's forward axis at the handle, the joints
                         before it share part of the turn (neck + head)
                pelvis   move / turn the pelvis itself (pinned chains hold)
       mirror   the opposite chain (mirror mode drags it mirrored; just for display)
       color    RGB of the handle
       bend     twobone only: where the lower bone swings when a straight limb starts to
                bend, in the root joint's frame (forearm forward: +X; shin back: -X); the
                elbow / knee handle (pole) overrides it once dragged
       extend   when the handle point (worn mesh) lies on a joint below the chain's end
                and the end is not a hand (see hand_branches), the chain grows by up to
                this many joints toward it: a pony's hoof below the wrist / cannon becomes
                the end bone (it takes the handle's orientation) and the wrist / cannon
                folds with the elbow and shoulder. 0 / absent: never (human legs: the
                foot is not a limb segment).
       max_bend twobone: how far a hinge may fold, degrees from straight (default 160)
       bento    1: an optional Bento chain (tail, ears, hind limbs), only shown with
                "Bento IK handles" (SLPoseStudioIKBentoChains) on
       forward  lookat only: the aiming axis in the last joint's frame (avatars face +X)
       share    lookat only: the part of the turn each joint before the last takes
     Chains whose joints the avatar does not have are skipped. Data-driven so pony /
     quadruped and human skeletons both work: edit or add chains here. -->
